452:\begin{defn} Throughout we let $t \in \mathbb{N} := \{1, 2, 3, \ldots \}$. Given two vertices $u, v \in V(G)$, the \textbf{distance} between $u$ and $v$, denoted $d(u,v)$, is the minimum length of the paths connecting $u$ and $v$. We say that $v \in V(G)$ is a \textbf{broadcasting vertex of transmission strength $t$} if $u$ transmits a \emph{signal} of strength $t - d(u,v)$ to every vertex $u \in V(G)$ with $d(u,v) < t$. In the case where $d(u,v)\geq t$, then the broadcast vertex of transmission strength $t$ does not transmit any signal to the vertex $u$.
